text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164703 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164706 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164709 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164713 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396253 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396256 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396259 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396263 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1071 "![](images/35/359b56411d4e215ef0ed423f6280a9e3641d5806d525b78e97569aaa02afe72f.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=8x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1071 "![](images/35/359b56411d4e215ef0ed423f6280a9e3641d5806d525b78e97569aaa02afe72f.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n${x^{′} = 8x - 4xy; y^{′} =-6y + 2xy$\nwhere $x = x(t), y = y(t)$, and $a, b, c$, and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\nThe trajectory\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}.\n\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{decreases}.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186175954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395835 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186175961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396413 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186176481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174994 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186176481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396348 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186176481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186176481 1071 "Let\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$. Then $T$ maps $R^{2}$ onto" 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186176481 1071 "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186176481 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,3)$ and $(2,6),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{6-3}{2-1}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=3x.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396334 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395936 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395949 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395962 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198471 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395908 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198471 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395926 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196111 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196117 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395898 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395904 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1071 "Describe the set $S = {…, 3, 6, 12, 24, 48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_{1} = 6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*\n\n$2^{n}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186198850 1071 "Describe the set $S={…,3,6,12,24,48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\nS={ {{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}} }\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1074 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,48,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=6,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in Z}\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197546 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186198951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396321 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186198951 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0<z<\\pi ,$ we have that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\ne 0.$ On the other hand, if $y-x=\\pi \\cdot n,$ with $n$ an integer, we obtain that $x>\\pi $ or $y>\\pi ,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396047 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396097 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396122 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396147 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396237 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396266 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396185 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396212 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186221442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396028 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220697 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220701 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220706 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396199 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396203 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396207 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{″}(s) = L{t^{2} \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $F^{″}(s) = \\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L3$: $F^{″}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{∂^{2}}{∂s^{2}}(e^{-st}f(t))dt$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{∂^{2}}{∂s^{2}}(e^{-st}f(t)) = t^{2}e^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $F^{″}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}t^{2}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L6$: $F^{″}(s) = L{t^{2} \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186229137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396226 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186229138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396275 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395707 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395706 1069 "q-10485" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395706 1071 "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1$ intersects the line $y=x.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395706 1072 #uuid "296c1691-3fdb-50aa-926f-7c3fb307cb51" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395706 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac