x=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396097 1072 #uuid "b4af4f8d-8886-57f9-92e8-20fda99f6128" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396097 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =4(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.027-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.108-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.099\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.1) & =4(0.1)^{3}-(0.1)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.001-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.004-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.003\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.099\\,9$ to $0.003\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.099\\,9}{0.003\\,9}=25.615\\,38…≈25.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈25.615\\,38… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(25.615\\,38…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈2.952… \\\\ p & ≈1.952….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396099 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396100 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396101 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396102 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396103 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1077 "field-3" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396103 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396098 1080 #uuid "1e9f3cc8-ee4d-5f7b-b815-f8923b3f6f94" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396099 1081 "20.865" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396099 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396099 1084 #uuid "8a98936b-f6f9-53c3-aefc-f49cb4b5c873" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396100 1081 "10.642" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396100 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396100 1084 #uuid "55c03e15-db65-5bda-97c7-68787c5792a7" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396101 1081 "27.015" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396101 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396101 1084 #uuid "427c0b3e-32c0-57c2-af3d-615ea8da505b" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396102 1081 "15.384" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396102 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396102 1084 #uuid "21e0a04d-f360-5d4e-bc3e-d30134ad9afc" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396103 1081 "25.6