,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396147 1072 #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396147 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396149 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396150 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396151 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396152 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396153 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1077 "field-2" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396150 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396148 1080 #uuid "d920cb15-c78d-5c51-9a36-297e1002feed" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396149 1081 "0.225" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396149 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396149 1084 #uuid "0b510a71-8c9b-527d-9bcb-f5fbb3d49293" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396150 1081 "0.003" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396150 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396150 1084 #uuid "fb33a210-7096-5874-a3f2-db5201cb5c83" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396151 1081 "0.281" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396151 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396151 1084 #uuid "7357b661-3a8e-586f-b745-87bada056b83" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396152 1081 "0.075" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396152 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396152 1084 #uuid "368eff5d-c023-5e5b-be1f-e7e458e23832" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396153 1081 "0.550" 13