ac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1))\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396239 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396240 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1077 "field-2" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396239 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1080 #uuid "c2ba4f30-23bd-5905-838d-76fd7661d248" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396239 1081 "almost linear" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396239 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396239 1084 #uuid "8cc0f169-e729-5947-84b4-6f07c528a50a" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396240 1081 "not almost linear" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396240 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533466 17592186