nd{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=\\lambda  \\\\ 2y=\\lambda  \\\\ 2z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$ \n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =12 \\\\ x+x+x & =12 \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,4),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,4)={4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}=48.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,5,2) & ={5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >f(4,4,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396336 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396337 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396338 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396339 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396340 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1077 "selection" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396339 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396335 1080 #uuid "7725e026-6e16-549e-8d1c-ac0a5575460e" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396336 1081 "64" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396336 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396336 1084 #uuid "3a12923a-fb30-547a-bfbe-786af7498fc6" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396337 1081 "36" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396337 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533466 1