186045585 true] [17592186396413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396413 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{3/2}$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{4}{15}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396413 1072 #uuid "4cc9f66e-fd87-5f99-8445-a69528565ab3" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396413 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{3/2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{4}{15}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{4}{15}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}{t}^{3/2}(\\frac{5}{3}-t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396415 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396416 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396417 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186396414 1075 #e